INT. TRUCK STOP DINER, ARM-WRESTLING TABLE - NIGHT

Steel table. Two stools. A crowd of truckers three-deep. Neon beer signs buzz.

BIG WENDELL TUCKER, forty, three hundred pounds, hands like shovels, grips the pegs. Across from him sits ADA PRZYBYLSKI, twenty-three, a barista in a hairnet, five-foot-two, sleeves rolled.

REFEREE
Ready.

Wendell grins. Ada doesn't. Their hands lock.

REFEREE (CONT'D)
Go.

Wendell surges. Ada's arm folds back to forty-five degrees and the crowd roars. Her face goes red, her knuckles white, and she exhales through clenched teeth. Wendell relaxes into the win.

ADA
Not yet.

She drives. The forearm rises, inch by inch, a hinge opening against a rusty spring, and Wendell's grin falters. The crowd quiets. His wrist cracks. She slams it back to vertical, then past, pressing it toward the steel, and he shouts, but she holds. He wrenches free, regrips, and with a bellow of rage reverses the angle, the table rocking. Ada's stool skids, her hairnet slips, a strand of hair falls into her mouth, and she bites it.

WENDELL
You can't!

ADA
I can.

The back of his hand touches steel. The room erupts. Wendell stares at his fist as though it belonged to a stranger.
